1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.300
Welcome to this short course,

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.300
Retrieval Optimization:
from Tokenization to Vector Quantization.

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.400
Built in partnership with Qdrant
and taught by Kacper Łukawski.

4
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.400
Retrieval augmented generation involves
two main steps.

5
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.500
First, a retriever searches
a large document corpus to find relevant

6
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.700
information.

7
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.000
Then a generator uses this information
to produce

8
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.700
accurate and contextually relevant results
for the user's query.

9
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.700
This course focus on enhancing
and optimizing the first step,

10
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:33.200
the retrieval
step, in your RAG and search applications.

11
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.833
You begin by learning
how tokenization is done

12
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:39.833
in large language models
and in embedding models,

13
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:43.533
and how the tokenizer can affect
the quality of your search.

14
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.633
Specifically, tokenizers
create a sequence of numerical ids

15
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:51.633
or integers representing the tokens

16
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:55.800
which usually correspond to words
or parts of words in a text.

17
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.233
The multiple ways to turn a piece of text
into a sequence of tokens.

18
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.600
For example, simple word

19
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.000
level tokenization would split a sentence
like "I enjoy learning."

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Into "I" and then "enjoy"
and "learning." While subword tokenization

21
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.300
can break it down
even further to "I" then "en"

22
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.300
"joy", "learn" and then "ing".

23
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.700
The tokenizer

24
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.700
is a key component of the language model
that is also trainable.

25
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.833
You learn about tokenization techniques
like wordpiece byte-pair encoding

26
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:36.933
and unigram tokenization, as well as how
token vocabulary impacts search results,

27
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.200
especially with special characters
like emojis, typos, and numerical values.

28
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.200
I'm delighted to introduce the instructor
for this course,

29
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.200
Kacper Łukawski, who is developer
relations lead for Qdrant. Kacper,

30
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:57.133
has been helping many developers create
and optimize the search and RAG systems.

31
00:01:57.533 --> 00:01:58.600
Thanks, Andrew.

32
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.433
The first step in optimizing your system

33
00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:03.700
is measuring the quality of its outputs.

34
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.833
Without this how can you, even tell
if your changes have made an improvement.

35
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.600
So in this course
you will learn how to assess

36
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.400
the quality of your search
using several quality metrics.

37
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.100
Vector databases
use specialized data structures

38
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.100
to approximate
the search of nearest neighbors.

39
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:27.300
HNSW which stands for Hierarchical,
Navigable, Small words,

40
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.200
is the most commonly used one,
and it has some parameters

41
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.800
that give you control over
how good the approximation is.

42
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.800
HNSW search is built
on top of a multi-layer graph,

43
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.000
and it's like shipping the package
in the mail.

44
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.000
The top layer gets
you close enough to the state

45
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.133
that mail should be delivered to,

46
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.700
the next layer then finds the city,

47
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.300
and as you go down
the layers, you get closer and closer.

48
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.000
You will see how to balance

49
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.400
the parameters used for forming
and searching the HNSW graph,

50
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:03.733
for higher speed and maximum relevance.

51
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.833
If you have millions of vectors a search,
then storing,

52
00:03:07.833 --> 00:03:12.000
indexing and searching can become resource
intensive and slow.

53
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.833
Say you built a news analysis
app that gathers

54
00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:18.833
all the news related to an industry
and summarizes them.

55
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.500
After chunking thousands of articles
published each day within a month,

56
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.800
you might easily end up
with millions of vector embeddings.

57
00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:31.133
If you end up with, say,
5 million vectors, then using OpenAI's

58
00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:35.200
Ada embedding model, which generates
a vector with a little over

59
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.600
a 1500 dimensions,
you need about 30 gigs of memory.

60
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.700
And this will continue to grow
every month.

61
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.700
This is where quantization techniques
come in.

62
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.600
Using the techniques you learned
in this course, you better reduce

63
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.333
the memory needed for your vector
search by up to 64x.

64
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.133
You will learn three main
quantization techniques.

65
00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:58.700
The first is product quantization.

66
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.833
This maps
subvectors to the nearest centroid,

67
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.533
reducing memory usage or increasing
indexing time.

68
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.033
The second is scalar quantization

69
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:12.100
by converting each float value
to 1-byte integers.

70
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.800
This significantly reduces memory
and speeds up both indexing

71
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.033
and search operations.

72
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:21.000
However, this hurts precision slightly.

73
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.500
And the third is an extreme
form of quantization called binary

74
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:29.000
quantization, which converts
float values to Boolean values,

75
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.500
which improves memory usage
significantly and improves search speed,

76
00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:37.100
but at even greater costs to precision.

77
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.600
In the next few lessons, you will first
learn about embedding models

78
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.633
and how they turn text into vectors.

79
00:04:44.333 --> 00:04:47.200
You will then learn how tokenization is done.

80
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.200
Next, you will look at practical issues
with tokenization

81
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.600
and how they can affect your vector
search and retrieval relevance.

82
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:57.300
You will also

83
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.233
learn how to measure the quality
of search results in RAG applications,

84
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.000
and why this is important
for making improvements.

85
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.300
We will review HNSW,

86
00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.600
and learn ways
to improve its search results.

87
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.633
We will also explore vector quantization
to reduce

88
00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:18.833
memory use
and make searches more efficient.

89
00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.600
By the end of this course,
you will know how to optimize semantic

90
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.400
search and build more reliable
AI applications.

91
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:30.533
Let's get started
and create something great.

92
00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:33.200
Many people have worked to create this
course.

93
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.200
I'd like to thank David Myriel
from Qdrant.

94
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.100
In addition, Esmaeil
Gargari and Geoff Ladwig from

95
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.100
DeepLearning.AI have also contributed
to this course.

96
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.500
Up first is a video on how embedding

97
00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.500
models turn text into vectors,
and the role of the tokenizer

98
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.033
in this whole process.

99
00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:55.433
I always thought tokenization
is one of the really critical,

100
00:05:55.433 --> 00:06:00.100
but often ignored and underappreciated
aspects of the models we use.

101
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.300
So let's go on to the next
video and learn about that.

